            <ArticleTitle>[A case of Raeder syndrome in a patient with known multiple sclerosis].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>347-9</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In 1918 the Norwegian neurologist Raeder was the first to describe a syndrome consisting of unilateral headache, facial pain and a Horner's syndrome which is incomplete; this syndrome is accompanied by multiple parasellar cranial nerve involvement. This syndrome was named &quot;Raeder's syndrome&quot; or paratrigeminal syndrome. A survey of the literature concerning &quot;Raeder's syndrome&quot; is given; there a two varieties of Raeder's syndrome, the migrainous or reflex type and the symptomatic type with multiple parasellar cranial nerve involvement. The first variety has a close relationship to cluster headache and to the so-called pericarotid syndrome. A case of an own patient suffering from multiple sclerosis and a migrainous type of Raeder's syndrome is described, assuming that his basic disease MS is independent of his Raeder's syndrome.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
